#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ~/.bash_github_tui
#
# Interactive GitHub TUI — fzf-powered screens
# Source this from .bashrc:
#   source ~/.bash_github_tui
#
# STATUS: working, and parked. Everything below runs; nothing here is
# half-finished. It is the next thing to work on, not the current one — see
# docs/decisions/github-tui.md for the plan, the open question that gates it,
# and the ideas not yet brought in. Keep that file and this header in step.
#
# Commands:
#   gh-tui        All GitHub features in a tui   (gh-ui is an alias)
#
# To dissolve into gh-tui — these are the screens, reachable directly for now:
#   _gpr           Interactive PR management (list → pick → act)
#   _gpr create    Create a new PR from current branch
#   _gpr mine      Show only your PRs
#   _ghsecrets     Manage repo & environment secrets
#   _ghbranch      Branches & rulesets (supersedes classic protection)
#   _ghenv         Environment management
#   _gha_ui        Interactive workflow picker → action menu → logs
#
# The non-interactive gh commands (gha, gha-fail, gha-open) and the shared
# helpers (__gh_check, __gh_repo, __gh_default_branch, _open_url) live in
# bash_github, which is sourced first. Everything here may use them. This file
# is the only one of the three that may invoke fzf, and CI enforces that — it
# is what keeps this layer replaceable as a unit.
#
# Navigation — nvim friendly. Encoded once in __fzf_menu, not repeated per
# call site; see bash_productivity.
#   Menus:  ↑↓ and hjkl navigate   enter select   -/q back
#   Lists:  type to filter   enter select   esc back
#   Pagers: r refresh   -/q back
#   Help:   ? in any screen shows that screen's page
#
# Dependencies: gh, fzf, jq

# ─── Per-screen help ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `?` in any screen shows that screen's help. This used to live in `h`
# in bash_productivity — 174 lines documenting screens in a different
# file, reachable only if you already knew to type `h gh-tui`. Help for
# an interactive tool belongs in the tool.
#
# Materialised to a file because fzf's ?:execute binding runs a command,
# not a shell function — the subshell fzf spawns has no access to these
# definitions. Written once per screen per session and cached.
_gh_help_file() {
  local screen="$1" dir="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/gh-tui-help-$$"
  mkdir -p "$dir" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  local f="$dir/$screen"
  [[ -s "$f" ]] || "_gh_help_$screen" > "$f" 2>/dev/null
  [[ -s "$f" ]] && echo "$f"
}

